\f0\fs24 \cf0 # Chapter 5 \'97 Assertions & Auto Waiting\
\
> **Goal:** Build reliable, deterministic, and self-healing assertions by leveraging Playwright's built-in auto-waiting and web-first assertions.\
\
---\
\
# Overview\
\
One of Playwright's biggest advantages over traditional browser automation frameworks is its built-in waiting mechanism.\
\
Unlike Selenium or WebDriverIO, Playwright automatically waits for elements to become actionable before interacting with them.\
\
Because of this, engineers should avoid implementing manual waits except in very specific scenarios.\
\
A stable automation framework relies on Playwright's synchronization mechanism rather than arbitrary delays.\
\
---\
\
# WAIT-001\
\
## Rule\
\
Never use `waitForTimeout()` as a synchronization strategy.\
\
## Severity\
\
Critical\
\
## Why\
\
Hard waits introduce:\
\
- Flaky tests\
- Longer execution time\
- Unnecessary delays\
- Random failures\
\
A timeout does not guarantee the application is ready.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(page.getByRole('button', \{\
    name: 'Save'\
\})).toBeVisible();\
```\
\
## Bad\
\
```ts\
await page.waitForTimeout(5000);\
\
await page.locator('#save').click();\
```\
\
## Exception\
\
Temporary debugging only.\
\
Must never be committed into the repository.\
\
## AI Rule\
\
Reject any implementation using waitForTimeout() for synchronization.\
\
---\
\
# WAIT-002\
\
## Rule\
\
Always use Playwright auto waiting.\
\
## Why\
\
Playwright automatically waits for:\
\
- Visible\
- Stable\
- Enabled\
- Receives Events\
\
before clicking.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await page.getByRole('button', \{\
    name: 'Login'\
\}).click();\
```\
\
No manual wait required.\
\
---\
\
# WAIT-003\
\
## Rule\
\
Do not wait before every action.\
\
## Bad\
\
```ts\
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);\
\
await username.fill("admin");\
\
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);\
\
await password.fill("password");\
```\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await username.fill("admin");\
\
await password.fill("password");\
\
await loginButton.click();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-001\
\
## Rule\
\
Prefer web-first assertions.\
\
## Why\
\
Playwright assertions automatically retry until timeout.\
\
They reduce flaky tests.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(\
    page.getByRole('heading')\
).toBeVisible();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-002\
\
## Rule\
\
Assert user-visible behavior.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(\
    page.getByText(\
        "Order created successfully"\
    )\
).toBeVisible();\
```\
\
## Avoid\
\
```ts\
expect(statusCode).toBe(200);\
```\
\
when the business requirement is validating UI success.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-003\
\
## Rule\
\
Use the most specific assertion possible.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(title)\
.toHaveText(\
    "Dashboard"\
);\
```\
\
## Less Specific\
\
```ts\
await expect(title)\
.toBeVisible();\
```\
\
Visible does not guarantee correctness.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-004\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toHaveText() when exact text matters.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(message)\
.toHaveText(\
    "Login successful"\
);\
```\
\
## Avoid\
\
```ts\
await expect(message)\
.toContainText(\
    "Login"\
);\
```\
\
if the full message is part of the requirement.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-005\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toContainText() when partial matching is acceptable.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(notification)\
.toContainText(\
    "Success"\
);\
```\
\
Useful when timestamps or IDs are dynamic.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-006\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toHaveValue() for input validation.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(emailInput)\
.toHaveValue(\
    "admin@mail.com"\
);\
```\
\
Avoid checking input values using text assertions.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-007\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toHaveURL() after navigation.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(page)\
.toHaveURL(\
    /dashboard/\
);\
```\
\
Avoid\
\
```ts\
expect(page.url())\
.toContain("dashboard");\
```\
\
The Playwright assertion retries automatically.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-008\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toHaveTitle() for page title validation.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(page)\
.toHaveTitle(\
    "Dashboard"\
);\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-009\
\
## Rule\
\
Prefer locator assertions over manual property extraction.\
\
## Bad\
\
```ts\
const text =\
await locator.textContent();\
\
expect(text)\
.toBe("Success");\
```\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(locator)\
.toHaveText(\
    "Success"\
);\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-010\
\
## Rule\
\
Never use boolean assertions on locators.\
\
## Bad\
\
```ts\
expect(\
    await button.isVisible()\
).toBeTruthy();\
```\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(button)\
.toBeVisible();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-011\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toBeHidden() instead of checking CSS styles.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(loader)\
.toBeHidden();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-012\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toBeEnabled() before business interaction when required.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(submitButton)\
.toBeEnabled();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-013\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toBeDisabled() when validating permissions.\
\
## Example\
\
Guest users cannot submit.\
\
```ts\
await expect(submitButton)\
.toBeDisabled();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-014\
\
## Rule\
\
Use toBeChecked() for checkbox and radio validation.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(rememberMe)\
.toBeChecked();\
```\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-015\
\
## Rule\
\
Use expect.soft() only when multiple validations should continue after failures.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect.soft(name)\
.toHaveText("John");\
\
await expect.soft(role)\
.toHaveText("Admin");\
\
await expect.soft(status)\
.toHaveText("Active");\
```\
\
Useful for reports.\
\
Not for critical flows.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-016\
\
## Rule\
\
Use expect.poll() for asynchronous backend operations.\
\
## Example\
\
```ts\
await expect.poll(async () => \{\
    return await orderApi.getStatus(orderId);\
\}).toBe("COMPLETED");\
```\
\
Useful when waiting for:\
\
- Queue\
- Worker\
- Background Job\
- Event Processing\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-017\
\
## Rule\
\
Do not duplicate assertions.\
\
## Bad\
\
```ts\
await expect(button)\
.toBeVisible();\
\
await expect(button)\
.toBeEnabled();\
\
await button.click();\
```\
\
If visibility already guarantees the button is actionable, extra assertions add noise.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-018\
\
## Rule\
\
Assertions belong inside test files.\
\
## Never\
\
```text\
LoginPage\
\
\uc0\u8595 \
\
clickLogin()\
\
\uc0\u8595 \
\
expect(...)\
```\
\
## Correct\
\
```text\
Test\
\
\uc0\u8595 \
\
LoginPage.login()\
\
\uc0\u8595 \
\
expect(...)\
```\
\
Page Objects perform actions.\
\
Tests perform validations.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-019\
\
## Rule\
\
Verify business outcomes, not implementation details.\
\
## Good\
\
```ts\
await expect(successToast)\
.toBeVisible();\
```\
\
## Avoid\
\
Checking hidden implementation values that users never see.\
\
---\
\
# ASSERT-020\
\
## Rule\
\
One assertion should validate one expectation.\
\
Avoid giant assertion blocks that verify unrelated behaviors.\
\
---\
\
# Common Mistakes\
\
| Mistake | Risk |\
|----------|------|\
| waitForTimeout() | Critical |\
| sleep() | Critical |\
| textContent() + expect() | Medium |\
| isVisible() + toBeTruthy() | Medium |\
| Assertions inside Page Objects | High |\
| Multiple unrelated assertions | Medium |\
| CSS validation instead of UI state | Medium |\
\
---\
\
# Decision Matrix\
\
| Requirement | Recommended Assertion |\
|-------------|----------------------|\
| Visible element | toBeVisible() |\
| Hidden element | toBeHidden() |\
| Exact text | toHaveText() |\
| Partial text | toContainText() |\
| URL | toHaveURL() |\
| Page title | toHaveTitle() |\
| Input value | toHaveValue() |\
| Checkbox | toBeChecked() |\
| Disabled button | toBeDisabled() |\
| Enabled button | toBeEnabled() |\
| Async backend | expect.poll() |\
| Continue after failures | expect.soft() |\
\
---\
\
# AI Coding Rules\
\
- Never use waitForTimeout() for synchronization.\
- Always rely on Playwright auto waiting.\
- Prefer Playwright assertions over manual value extraction.\
- Use locator assertions instead of boolean assertions.\
- Keep assertions inside test files.\
- Validate business outcomes.\
- Use exact assertions whenever possible.\
- Use partial assertions only when dynamic values exist.\
- Use expect.poll() for asynchronous backend operations.\
- Use expect.soft() only for non-blocking validations.\
\
---\
\
# Enterprise Checklist\
\
- [ ] No waitForTimeout()\
- [ ] Uses Playwright auto waiting\
- [ ] Assertions are inside tests\
- [ ] Uses locator assertions\
- [ ] No manual polling loops\
- [ ] Uses toHaveURL() after navigation\
- [ ] Uses toHaveText() when exact text matters\
- [ ] Uses expect.poll() for async processing\
- [ ] Validates business outcomes\
- [ ] Tests remain deterministic\
\
---\
\
# Summary\
\
Playwright's built-in auto-waiting and web-first assertions eliminate most synchronization problems found in older automation frameworks.\
\
**Golden Rules**\
\
1. Never use `waitForTimeout()` for synchronization.\
2. Trust Playwright's auto-waiting.\
3. Prefer locator assertions over manual checks.\
4. Keep assertions inside test files.\
5. Validate user-visible business outcomes rather than implementation details.}